          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nYour clinician's words may refer to a few different situations, and the guidance I found describes how they are told apart.\n\nDoctors usually start with a blood test for thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). If that is raised, they also measure free thyroxine, the thyroid hormone itself. Low free thyroxine with a raised TSH is called **overt hypothyroidism**. A raised TSH with normal free thyroxine is called **subclinical hypothyroidism**. Antibody tests can show whether an immune condition is the cause. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*, endocrinology guidance.)\n\nSymptoms are common and often mild, so they do not confirm the condition alone. They can include tiredness, feeling cold, weight gain, constipation, dry skin, thinning hair, muscle aches, heavy or irregular periods, low mood, and slowed thinking. Because these symptoms are so common, a diagnosis is usually confirmed with repeated blood tests rather than one result. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe passages I found do not cover treatment, what your particular results mean, or how often you should be tested. Your clinician is the right person for those questions. Useful things to ask include which test results led to the diagnosis, whether it is overt or subclinical, and when your next blood test should be.",
