          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often do they recheck it?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe guidance I found covers one specific case. It says a thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) test is rechecked **six to eight weeks after starting levothyroxine, or after any change in dose**, because it takes that long for the level to settle. (Source: *Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change*, endocrinology guidance.)\n\nThe same guidance says an abnormal result is usually repeated after several weeks before long-term treatment starts. This is because thyroid levels can shift with illness and through the day.\n\nThe passages I found do not say how often people are tested if they are not on levothyroxine, or how often a subclinical result is rechecked. Ask your clinician when your next test is due, and whether it will be done before any treatment decision.",
